      "latin": "iterō, iterāre, iterāvī, iterātum",
      "status": "recommended",
      "period": "classical",
      "gloss": "say or do a second time, repeat",
      "grammar": "verb, first conjugation, transitive",
      "evidence": [
        "Cic. Att. 14.14.1: <span lang=\"la\">Iterādum eadem ista mihi.</span>",
        "Liv. 1.45.2 (Lewis &amp; Short): <span lang=\"la\">saepe iterandō eadem</span>"
      ],
      "comment": "From <span lang=\"la\">iterum</span>, “a second time”: to say the same words again, or do a thing again (<span lang=\"la\">pugnam iterāre</span>, Liv. 6.32). In farming it is to plough a second time (Lewis &amp; Short)."
    },
    {
      "latin": "repetō, repetere, repetīvī, repetītum",
      "status": "alternative",
      "period": "classical",
      "gloss": "go over again, repeat; resume",
      "grammar": "verb, third conjugation, transitive",
      "evidence": [
        "Traupman 34: <span lang=\"la\">repete quid modo dīxerīs</span>, repeat what you just said",
        "Cic. Fin. 5.51 (Lewis &amp; Short): <span lang=\"la\">praetermissa repetimus</span>",
        "Traupman 31"
      ],
      "comment": "To go back over what was said or begun, so also to resume; Traupman’s classroom word. Its first senses are to attack again and to go back for, fetch, or claim back (<span lang=\"la\">rēs repetere</span>); Lewis &amp; Short."
    },
